  "narrative": {
    "northStar": "The Spool Label",
    "overview": "The site is set like the label on a filament spool: a dark spool, die-cut bone labels, a loud color swatch, huge material codes, and small spec rows that tell you exactly what you're getting. It is industrial and plain-spoken, the way a good label is, and it lets the owner's prints carry the color.\n\nSignal orange is the swatch. It fills the band under the hero and the footer, and the top edge of both bands is the Franklin Mountains seen from central El Paso, the one local landmark, used as a shape rather than an illustration. The same orange marks every action. Bone label stock carries the things a visitor reads closely or fills in: the photo label in the hero, the four material labels, and the quote form. Kraft sand prints the small keys on the dark ground.\n\nMotion is printing. The hero photo prints in 48 visible layers inside its label with a layer counter on the label's foot; the mountain band, gallery photos, and material labels print up from the bed when they enter. Everything is visible without JavaScript or motion.",
    "keyCharacteristics": [
      "Dark spool-black ground, bone label stock, one orange swatch, kraft sand keys.",
      "Die-cut labels: 8px corners, solid fills, no borders, no shadows.",
      "Condensed 800 uppercase for headlines and material codes; tracked small-caps keys over values.",
      "Orange bands whose top edge is the Franklin ridge open and close the page.",
      "Owner photos only."
    ],
    "rules": [
      {
        "name": "The Swatch Rule",
        "body": "Orange is the swatch: actions, the two mountain bands, and active states. It never colors headline words.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Stock Rule",
        "body": "Bone stock is for things read closely or filled in. On bone, text is spool black, keys are Stock Ink Soft, and errors are Orange Ink.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Key-Over-Value Rule",
        "body": "Small facts are a key over a value, like a spec row. No eyebrow labels above headings.",
        "section": "typography"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Die-Cut Rule",
        "body": "A label is a solid fill with 8px corners. No borders around labels, no shadows, and no colored stripes along a card's edge.",
        "section": "elevation"
      }
    ],
    "dos": [
      "**Do** keep to the inks: spool black (#14120e), bone stock (#ede7db), signal orange (#ff6b1a), kraft sand (#cfae82).",
      "**Do** set facts as a key (0.75rem, 700, tracked, uppercase) over a value.",
      "**Do** use 8px corners on labels and 4px on controls.",
      "**Do** keep motion to \"printing\": stepped reveals from the bottom up, and everything visible under reduced motion.",
      "**Do** write every string twice, English then Spanish, as sibling `lang` elements."
    ],
    "donts": [
      "**Don't** add shadows, glows, gradients, or neon edges. The owner ruled out a sci-fi look.",
      "**Don't** put colored stripes along the top or side of a card.",
      "**Don't** color headline words orange.",
      "**Don't** use stock photos. Only the owner's prints.",
      "**Don't** soften into crafty or cute styling (pastels, doodles, script fonts)."
    ]
